=== the identity path: privileged steps (NOT executed here) ===
This build does NOT need these steps: capability comes from the measured
confined-caller deployment (node tooling/probes/v12-confined-caller.mjs),
which uses no elevation. They remain here for a host that wants a separate
broker ACCOUNT on top of it, and elevation stays a human decision. On Windows x64:
  1. create a restricted worker identity (as Administrator, once):
       net user canary-worker <STRONG-PASSWORD> /add /passwordreq:yes
       net localgroup "Users" canary-worker /add
  2. install the broker/supervisor to run as ANOTHER identity (NOT the worker):
       sc.exe create CanaryBroker binPath= "<install>\canary-broker.exe" obj= "<BROKER-ACCOUNT>" start= auto
       sc.exe start CanaryBroker
  3. give the provider store a DACL that excludes the worker identity:
       icacls "C:\ProgramData\Canary" /inheritance:r /grant:r "<BROKER-ACCOUNT>:(OI)(CI)F" /grant:r "SYSTEM:(OI)(CI)F"
  4. declare the pairing, then re-run THIS probe and the broker contract suite:
       set CANARY_WORKER_USER=canary-worker
       set CANARY_BROKER_ENDPOINT=\\.\pipe\canary-broker
       node tooling/probes/provider-boundary.mjs && npm test
Until step 4 passes on a host with a real second identity, the IDENTITY path contributes
no control; that is not the same as HARDENED being unreachable, which it is not:
HARDENED is produced by a measured confined-caller deployment, and this probe checks
that a host without one still reports every control unavailable.
